rature, whether that variation correlates with performance differences, and how well the F2 constraint quality predicts outcomes on a per-problem basis. These give me concrete numbers to compare across best, median, and worst cases.\n\nI'm also thinking about aggregate measures—the overall weighted gap across all experiments, whether any single experiment hits statistical significance, how many distinct C0 levels emerge as model scale changes, and what the constraint ablation reveals about MaxSAT versus simpler alternatives. The constraint ablation from exp-013 already shows those methods are roughly equivalent, so that's settled. What's still uncertain is the temperature ablation results coming in, the quality of the F2 per-problem data, whether the scaling experiments will run, and whether there are any replication issues to account for. Before I write the YAML structure, I should clarify what \"worst case\" actually means for the paper's narrative.\n\nThe user's worst-case scenario still targets EMNLP but reframes the story—instead of a structural \"capability trap,\" it becomes an empirical analysis of when symbolic consensus methods are actually useful. Let me map out the three scenarios: best case has all three claims holding with temperature confirming the structural trap and F2 showing a compelling per-problem pattern plus scaling evidence for the diagnostic claim. Median case is where claims start to weaken.\n\nFor the median scenario, claims 1 and 2 still hold but claim 3 becomes just a binary indicator rather than a gradient. The temperature ablation mostly checks out except for some noise, F2 needs binned visualization instead of raw scatter, and C0 is binary rather than continuous—so it works as an early stopping criterion but I can't claim it's a true predictor. I'd compensate by expanding model coverage to strengthen the narrative.\n\nIn the worst case, the temperature ablation actually shows SICA helps at high temperatures, so the trap isn't \"regard